course: dictionary
lesson: 12208
title: Congregate: coming together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To congregate means to come together and form a group or crowd in one place. «Every morning the pupils congregate in the playground before the bell rings.» The clue is that many pupils are in one place, so the word must mean they gather together.
explain: Animals can congregate too. «When the dry season comes, zebras congregate around the waterhole.» The zebras are not just visiting the waterhole. They are gathering there in a big group, so congregate tells us many animals are coming together.
explain: Congregate also has a church meaning. «On Sunday, the villagers congregate in the church for the service.» Here it means to gather for worship. The people who gather are called the congregation. Everyday words like gather and meet can often replace congregate, so try them in the sentence to check.
fact: Congregate means to come together and form a group or crowd in one place.
fact: Everyday words with a similar meaning are gather and meet.
fact: In church, to congregate means to gather for worship, and the gathered people are called the congregation.
fact: Animals can congregate too, such as at waterholes or in flocks.
fact: Congregate is a formal word, so you will see it more often in books and speeches than in everyday chat.
exercise: «The pupils congregate in the playground before the bell.» What does congregate mean? | Sit down alone | *Come together in one place | Run away quickly | Sing loudly
exercise: Which everyday word could replace congregate in «The villagers congregate for the service»? | argue | sleep | *gather | vanish
exercise: Which word names the people who gather together for worship in church? | pulpit | steeple | *congregation | choir
test: «Zebras congregate around the waterhole in the dry season.» What does congregate mean here? | *Come together in a group | Run far away | Drink all the water | Fall asleep
test: Which meaning of congregate fits «People congregate in church on Sunday»? | Dance in a circle | Ring the bell loudly | Sell books at the door | *Gather together for worship
exam: Which sentence uses congregate correctly? | Pupils congregate a sandwich | Pupils congregate the hall at lunchtime | *Pupils congregate in the hall at lunchtime | Pupils congregates the book
